lesson g3-adding-in-columns v=1 format=worked grade=3 unit=1 subject=maths levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Adding in columns"
  goal "Add three-digit numbers in columns, carrying where the column is full, and use adding to solve problems."

  example {
    worked columns.add-three-digit a=347 b=285 easy-level=medium hard-level=medium
    say """
      Seven and five is twelve: two ones and a ten carried. Four tens, eight tens and the carried
      ten make thirteen tens, so three tens and a hundred carried.
      """
  }
  exercises {
    practice columns.add-three-digit count=3 hard-count=2 seed=7
    practice add.bar-missing-part count=2 seed=4 easy-seed=9
    practice add.library-books count=2 seed=9
    practice columns.add count=1 seed=3
    level hard {
      practice columns.missing-digits-add count=1 seed=2
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show columns.missing-digits-add p=2 x=4 c=8 y=3 e=6 f=5 easy-x=3 easy-e=4 easy-y=4 hard-p=3 hard-c=7 hard-y=2 hard-e=5 hard-f=8
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show add.biggest-total a=1 b=3 c=4 d=6 e=7 f=8 hard-c=5 hard-f=9
  }

  grown-ups """
    Ask what the carried digit is worth each time. Ten, then a hundred, and saying so out loud is
    what keeps the method from becoming a trick. The bar questions have the missing number in the
    middle of the story, so the sum is not the answer: ask which part is missing before any
    working. In the two-star question the ones column decides whether a ten is carried, and that
    decides the hidden tens digit. In the three-star question, a child who puts 8, 7 and 6 into one
    number has made a good first try; ask what happens if the 8 and the 7 go in different numbers.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level only the ones carry in the column sums to do and the four-digit sum, the missing part of the bar is found with no exchange, the library lends out fewer than a hundred books, the hidden digits sit in columns that do not carry, and the cards are as written."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the tens carry as well as the ones, every column of the four-digit sum carries, the missing part of the bar needs a hundred exchanged, the library numbers are larger, the hidden digits are asked twice with the tens carrying too and, in the second, a zero in the total, and the cards are 1, 3, 5, 6, 7 and 9, the same search with a bigger total."
  }
}
